Rodney, in my games, shows up something like 0-5 times (in
addition to the two guaranteed contacts) per game. Monsters
may appear as a "Wizard effect" any time after you've killed
the Wizard, his presence is not necessary, but he may cast
that spell as well I think.

You can tackle the Wizard and observe all that nastiness;
but you shouldn't do that. You should position yourself so
that the Wizard is standing between a wall and yourself and
(mostly) a single zap will instantly kill him - I always
wondered why in Nethack this has been made that simple and
effective. The Wizard is also not stoning resistant which
makes his summon-nasties-spell fire back at him. Of course
there are other means as well, e.g. a tame levelled up
purple worm, but since the trivial means already suffice
we don't need to consider many other options. Though if
you delay the home-run the Wizard may appear more often,
so that you'd need more cockatrices or more wands of death
(or charging scrolls), well, that's on you to control.

The Wizard is not a wimp only if you *allow* him to be The
Boss! - Well, this is *a bit* exaggerated, but you got the
point, I suppose. :-)

> 
>> and the special effects of the Riders complicate matters.
> 
> I was expecting problems from that, but either it's
> overrated or I got incredibly lucky. 

(You gave the explanation for your special case below.)

> I did have to
> apply my unicorn horn a couple of times and eat
> a K-ration, but it wasn't a big deal. 

You shouldn't rely on the unicorn horn in case of Pestilence;
that is what's often had been suggested by many folks here.

Famine is not primarily a problem alone because of the hunger
he inflicts; since you can and should satiate yourself before
starting the final Astral level. The stunning effect will (in
addition to other attacks) contribute to problems. Famine is
generally considered to be the easiest Rider to handle.

Both Riders are susceptible to a death ray, though, so there
is no need to get in melee range. Entering melee is only a
sane option if you can kill those Riders with 1-2 hits. But
otherwise you need them weakened, say by using conflict, or
by preparing the melee with a missile-storm.

> On the whole,
> I'd rather face down all three riders at the same
> time than have to fight the Wizard of Yendor.

The problem is that you seem to try to melee the Wizard to
death, which is a really bad tactics. The other issue is
that you can only assume two-weaponing of two +max weapons
in specific cases. If you play other character classes you
will have to be more creative (not much more, but anyway).

> 
>> You need some good preparation there; best, IME,
>> are wands of teleport, spell of jumping, and wands
>> of death.  Highly enchanted multi-fire missile weapons
>> are also good, specifically to deal with the Riders.
> 
> I had all of that.  I used none of it.  I just smacked
> them upside the head with my sword and they died
> after like two hits -- MUCH easier than the Wizard.
> 
> Granted, I was a moderately overprepared Valkyrie
> dual-wielding +7 Excalibur with a +6 silver saber.

Now imagine what you'd have done if you hadn't that weapons
and class specific weapon proficiency.

> I suppose if I'd been a speed-running neutral wizard
> I might've had more issues with the riders. 

The Wizard has the magic missile spell (if not the spell
of death). A character of any class that managed to get the
Amulet will have of be able to apply some means to handle
the Riders, if only the option to circumvent (and not meet)
them.

> But I
> don't think I have the skills to get the Book from
> the Wizard with a weak character, so how would
> I even reach the astral plane? 

You really don't need any skills for the Wizard! A fresh
c-corpse from the zoo, a wand of death from Orcus, and
some more not so common means will all give you what you
desire. Often I don't even *see* the Wizard when I kill
him; zap wand of digging into his inner tower, zap a wand
of death, enter and get the book from the dead corpse.

> (I didn't say the
> riders were totally harmless, only that they're
> anticlimactic after dealing with the Wizard a few
> times on the way back up from the Sanctum.)

You just shouldn't deal with the Wizard unnecessarily!

>> You can prepare yourself so that the Riders are not
>> much of a threat. Their HP's are usually not too high,
> 
> Yeah, I definitely noticed their unimpressive HP.  It was
> like killing mid-level monsters.  Whack, whack, done.
> I've met quadrupeds that were harder to kill.

I think it would get more interesting if the death ray
would be ineffective against them, and if they have so
many HPs that even a combat adventurer like the one that
you depicted above needs at least three hits/contacts;
then their special attacks would become more effective
or even visible.

But again, avoiding them is the overall best tactics to
do Astral (and not only Astral). That's why jumping and
a wand of teleport (or just conflict) is so good for
*any* class.

> 
>> and two of them are susceptible to the wand of death
> 
> Waste of a wand charge, IMO.  Save it for the high
> priest, or the wizard if he shows up again.

The [high] priests can be immune to the death ray due to
the possible cloak of MR - happened in some of my games.

The Wizard can not show up again on the Astral Plane; all
charges that you have you can spend on the Riders or what
else you think would be helpful. (While not yet on Astral
it's also one of the best choices for the Wizard, yes.)

